cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use NucLogic, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "NucLogic"
type: software
abstract: >-
  Nuclear segmentation in 3D microscopy remains challenging, particularly for
  low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) data from dense tissue samples, timelapse
  imaging, or in vivo experiments. Yet accurate 3D nuclear segmentation is a
  prerequisite for quantitative single-cell analysis, making this a key
  bottleneck for high-throughput study of challenging datasets. We present
  NucLogic, a fast and robust 3D nuclear segmentation algorithm that combines
  the maturity of 2D segmentation with geometric stitching rules based on
  nuclear shape, orientation, and intensity, to reconstruct accurate 3D volumes.
  NucLogic reliably segments challenging datasets including long-term timelapse
  imaging of organoids, and large dense structures such as the zebrafish brain.
  Benchmarked against other state-of-the-art segmentation tools, NucLogic
  matches F1 scores at high SNR, with increasing outperformance as SNR
  decreases. Importantly, segmentation completes at 20–40% of the computation
  time, with greater speed gains on larger images. Beyond segmentation, NucLogic
  includes an intuitive graphical user interface allowing non-experts to
  segment, quantify, and visualize 3D and 4D data. This enables straightforward
  quantification of cell numbers, alongside integration of a wide range of both
  cell-intrinsic properties, such as phenotype and morphology, and local
  environment metrics, such as density and spatial interactions. Together,
  NucLogic makes quantitative 3D nuclear analysis of previously unmanageable
  datasets accessible to a broad research community.
